0. Protective and restraining orders

1. For operational response to a fact of violence against women and/or domestic violence, the authorized body may issue a restraining order or a protective order as a temporary measure to protect the victim and restrict certain actions of the perpetrator.

1[1]. The issuance of a restraining order as a temporary measure by an authorized police officer to ensure the protection of the victim and the restriction of certain actions of the perpetrator is mandatory in cases of commencement or ongoing investigation under Articles 108, 109, 115, 117, 118, 120, 126, 137 and 138, Article 143, Part 3, subparagraph "e" and Part 4, Article 144, Part 2, subparagraph "i" and Part 3, Articles 144[1]–144[3] and 151, Article 160, Part 2, subparagraph "a" and Part 3, and Article 381[1] of the Criminal Code of Georgia.

1 2. After receiving a notification about a fact of violence against women and/or domestic violence provided for in the first paragraph of this article, if there are no grounds for commencing an investigation and, accordingly, the procedure established by paragraph 1 1 of this article is not applied, the authorized police officer shall assess the risk of recurrence of violence against women and/or domestic violence in accordance with the procedure established by the order of the Minister of Internal Affairs of Georgia and, depending on the severity of this risk, shall make one of the following decisions:

a) on the issuance of a restraining order;

b) on the refusal to issue a restraining order.

2. A protective order is an act issued by a first instance court (judge) through administrative proceedings, which determines temporary measures for the protection of the victim.

3. A restraining order is an act issued by an authorized police officer, which determines temporary measures for the protection of the victim in cases of violence against women and/or domestic violence. A restraining order is issued on the basis of a risk assessment questionnaire defined by the restraining order protocol approved by the order of the Minister of Internal Affairs of Georgia.

3 1. A restraining order shall indicate:

a) the date and place of its issuance;

b) the circumstances that formed the basis for its issuance;

c) the perpetrator's name, surname, date and place of birth, personal number, profession and place of residence;

c 1) the victim's name, surname, date of birth and personal number;

d) the list of actions provided for in paragraph 3 2 of this article, the performance of which is prohibited to the perpetrator.

3 2. A restraining order shall be signed by the person authorized to issue it, the victim and the perpetrator. A restraining order may provide for:

a) the issue of removing the perpetrator from the victim's dwelling, regardless of whether the perpetrator is the owner of this dwelling or not;

b) the issues of separating the victim and persons dependent on them from the perpetrator and placing them in a shelter;

c) the prohibition of the perpetrator's right to use jointly owned property individually;

d) the issue of separating the perpetrator from a minor;

e) the issue of the perpetrator's approach to the victim, their workplace and other places where the victim is located;

v) the issue of implementing electronic surveillance;

z) the restriction or prohibition of the perpetrator's right to use weapons (including service weapons) during the period of validity of this order or during the period determined by this order, the prohibition of the right to acquire weapons or to obtain a permit or license for their acquisition, and the conditions for storing or temporarily confiscating weapons in personal possession and/or owned weapons (including service weapons);

t) a warning to the perpetrator about the imposition of liability established by the legislation of Georgia in case of failure to fulfill the requirements and obligations provided for by this order;

i) other issues, the resolution of which is necessary to ensure the safety of the victim.

3 3. If the victim and/or the perpetrator for any reason cannot sign the restraining order, it shall be signed only by the person authorized to issue the restraining order. In this case, an appropriate entry shall be made in the restraining order and the reason for the victim's and/or perpetrator's refusal to sign/inability to sign shall be indicated.

3 4. A restraining order may indicate one, several or all of the issues provided for in paragraph 3 2 of this article, adequate to the fact of violence. A restraining order must indicate the restriction of the right to use weapons defined by subparagraph "v" of paragraph 3 2 of this article in cases of commencement or ongoing investigation under Articles 108, 109, 115, 117, 118, 120, 126, 137 and 138, Article 143, Part 3, subparagraph "e", Article 144, Part 2, subparagraph "i", Articles 144 1–144 3 and 151, Article 160, Part 2, subparagraph "a" and Part 3, and Article 381 1 of the Criminal Code of Georgia.

3 5. A restraining order shall be written in the language of the proceedings, clearly and comprehensibly. It may be written by hand or printed using technical means.

3 6. A restraining order shall enter into force upon issuance. Within 24 hours it shall be sent/delivered to the victim and the perpetrator, and one copy of the restraining order shall remain with the issuing body.

3 7. A copy of the restraining order shall be immediately sent to:

a) the Ministry of Internal Affairs of Georgia:

a.a) the relevant district service of the territorial body according to the victim's place of residence, for the purpose of responding to violations of the requirements and obligations provided for by the restraining order;

a.b) the relevant structural subdivision, for the purpose of entering information into the appropriate electronic database, rapid and effective response to violations, and recording statistical data;

a.c) the public law legal entity – the Service Agency of the Ministry of Internal Affairs of Georgia, if the restraining order concerns issues related to weapons;

a.d) the public law legal entity of the Ministry of Internal Affairs of Georgia – the Public Safety Management Center "112" (hereinafter – LEPL - "112"), if the restraining order provides for the implementation of electronic surveillance;

b) the relevant local guardianship and trusteeship body, if the issue provided for by the restraining order concerns a minor.

3 . The victim or the perpetrator may appeal the restraining order within 3 days from the date of delivery/receipt of this order.

3 . If, during the consideration of the issue of issuing a restraining order, signs of a crime provided for by the Criminal Code of Georgia are identified, in parallel with the consideration of the issue of issuing a restraining order, the case materials shall be sent to the relevant body according to jurisdiction to decide the issue of criminal prosecution;

3 10. In case of establishing electronic surveillance of the perpetrator, the parameters of the geographical zones where the approach provided for by subparagraph "e" of paragraph 3 2 of this article is prohibited shall be determined by the order of the Minister of Internal Affairs of Georgia.

4. Failure by the perpetrator to fulfill the requirements provided for by a restraining or protective order, as well as disobedience to a decision made by a social worker on the separation of a minor, shall entail liability in accordance with the procedure established by the legislation of Georgia.

5. The application of measures (criminal mechanisms) provided for by the criminal legislation of Georgia against a person who has committed violence against women and/or domestic violence (perpetrator) does not prevent the issuance of a protective order or a restraining order to ensure the protection of the person injured as a result of violence (victim).

6. If criminal prosecution has been initiated against a person who has committed violence against women and/or domestic violence (perpetrator) on charges of violence against women and/or domestic violence and/or domestic crime and a non-custodial preventive measure has been applied, the authorized court shall consider and decide the issue of applying restrictive measures against the perpetrator to ensure the protection of the person injured as a result of violence (victim) through criminal proceedings.

7. A restraining order must provide for the perpetrator's completion of a mandatory training course oriented towards changing violent attitudes and behavior.

